-- title:
--   Almost-everywhere convergence of the discretized jump integrand on the whole product space
-- statement:
--   Let $(h_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be a family of meshes indexed by a filter $l$, with $h_i > 0$ for every $i$
--   and $h_i \to 0$ along $l$. Fix $\alpha, R_0 \in \mathbb{R}$, a centre $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and a
--   radius $R \in \mathbb{R}$, let $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be locally integrable, and assume
--   $d > 0$. With
--
--   * $j_\alpha(s,t) = \lVert s-t\rVert^{-d-\alpha}$ the jump kernel `jumpKernel d α`;
--   * $f_h(u) = h^{-d}\int_{\bar A_h(u)} f$ the cube-average step approximation `cubeAvg d h f`, taken
--     over the closed cube $\bar A_h(u) = \{x : \max_i|x_i - u_i| \le h/2\}$;
--   * $x_h(s) = \big(\operatorname{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\big)_i$ the step index of the tiling at mesh $h$
--     (the nearest point of $h\mathbb{Z}^d$);
--   * $C_h$ the summand `discreteC d h (ball x₀ R) (R₀·h) (jumpKernel d α) f_h` of the discrete form,
--
--   $$C_h(x,y) = \mathbf 1_{D_h}(x,y)\,\big(f_h(x)-f_h(y)\big)^2 j_\alpha(x,y),
--   \quad D_h = \big\{(x,y) \in \big(B_R(x_0)\cap h\mathbb{Z}^d\big)^2 : R_0 h < \lVert x-y\rVert\big\},$$
--
--   one has: for almost every pair $p = (p_1,p_2) \in \mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d$,
--
--   $$C_{h_i}\big(x_{h_i}(p_1),\,x_{h_i}(p_2)\big) \;\longrightarrow\;
--   \mathbf{1}_{B_R(x_0)\times B_R(x_0)}(p)\,\big(f(p_1)-f(p_2)\big)^2\,j_\alpha(p_1,p_2)
--   \qquad \text{along } l .$$
--
--   This is the unguarded form of the previous convergence statement: the limit is the $H^{\alpha/2}$
--   integrand of the ball, extended by zero to all of $\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d$. Stated this way
--   it applies directly to Fatou's lemma over the whole product space, with no case distinction at the
--   boundary — outside the closed ball the lattice index is eventually outside the ball too, so the
--   discretized integrand is eventually zero, and the sphere itself is a null set.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The indicator on the right is the Lean `Set.indicator` of the product ball,
--   so the limit function is literally the extension by zero. As before no sign or size condition is
--   imposed on $\alpha$, $R_0$ or $R$; all values are in `ℝ≥0∞`.
